papers/69-orientation-sensitive-surface-flat-sft/rounds/GPT54_XHIGH_ROUND1_RESOLUTION.md:27:| Step 4 used the known-base Vandermonde lemma at index zero without stating that case, and blurred the recovered coefficient with the signed multiplicity difference | Lemma 5.1 now permits any \(r\) consecutive nonnegative indices, explicitly including \(m_0=0\). Theorem 5.2 now uses \(R_0,\ldots,R_{r-1}\), recovers \(b_d=(c_d^+-c_d^-)/d\), and only then multiplies by the already recovered \(d\) to obtain \(\delta_d=c_d^+-c_d^-\). | `sections/5_moment_recovery.tex`, `sections/1_introduction.tex`, `PROOF_PACKAGE.md`, `CLAIMS_EVIDENCE.md`, `NARRATIVE_REPORT.md` |
papers/29-functorial-chiral-counterterm-no-go/NARRATIVE_REPORT.md:13:The second obstruction is selectivity.  A diagonal counterterm has no pair frequency and cannot alter the mixed cosine ledger.  Once the class is widened to pair-local rules linear in the native Gram contraction, the coefficient must either preserve a local nonzero pair mechanism or cancel it.  Matched arithmetic and control pairs present the same local input, so those two requirements are inconsistent.  Exact mutated, composite-only, generic-DAG, and random-inventory controls realize the obstruction.
papers/29-functorial-chiral-counterterm-no-go/NARRATIVE_REPORT.md:34:The local no-go is not universal.  An isomorphism-invariant coefficient may inspect the entire filtered tower and recognize its global isomorphism class.  Such a construction can act as an inventory oracle while remaining formally natural.  This paper neither endorses it nor proves it impossible.  Excluding it requires an independently motivated locality, continuity, or additivity axiom.
papers/29-functorial-chiral-counterterm-no-go/NARRATIVE_REPORT.md:41:\(-2C_\eta\gamma\).  Diagonal schemes preserve every mixed frequency.  More generally, no quadratic, additive, pair-local rule linear in the native Gram contraction can preserve the divisibility mixed mechanism while cancelling matched non-arithmetic controls.  This is a scoped theorem: bare naturality still permits nonlocal filtered-tower invariants.  We keep the nonexistent ordinary quadratic trace, the honest third-regularized Fredholm determinant, and a new scheme-dependent renormalized functional under separate ownership.  The latter differs between schemes by a zero-free exponential and does not change the auxiliary determinant divisor.  Exact relabel, cutoff, mutated, composite-only, generic-DAG, and random-inventory controls support the bounded no-go.  The strict arithmetic-spectral route remains rejected.
